VPC deep dive : -

𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝗮 𝗩P𝗖?

A VPC is your private network inside AWS.
Every resource you create — EC2, RDS, EKS nodes, Lambda — lives inside a VPC.
Without a VPC, nothing talks to anything.

But the part most engineers get wrong isn't the VPC itself.
It's what lives inside it.

𝗣𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗶𝗰 𝘀𝘂𝗯𝗻𝗲𝘁 𝘃𝘀 𝗣𝗿𝗶𝘃𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝘀𝘂𝗯𝗻𝗲𝘁

Public subnet = has a route to the Internet Gateway.
Resources here can reach the internet AND be reached from the internet.
Use for: Load balancers, bastion hosts, NAT Gateways.

Private subnet = no route to the Internet Gateway.
Resources here are invisible to the internet.
Use for: EKS nodes, RDS, application servers, Lambda in VPC.

The most common mistake: putting EKS nodes or RDS in a public subnet.
At Citi Bank, every application resource lived in private subnets. Non-negotiable for banking compliance.

𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝗮 𝗡𝗔𝗧 𝗚𝗮𝘁𝗲𝘄𝗮𝘆?

Private resources still need to reach the internet sometimes — to pull packages, call external APIs, download updates.
But they can't go directly through the Internet Gateway — that would expose them.

Solution: NAT Gateway sits in the PUBLIC subnet.
Private resources route outbound traffic through NAT.
NAT reaches the internet on their behalf.
Inbound connections from the internet? Still blocked. Private resources stay invisible.

Outbound only. One way. Secure.

𝗦𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗶𝘁𝘆 𝗚𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗽𝘀 𝘃𝘀 𝗡𝗔𝗖𝗟𝘀

Security Groups = stateful firewall at the resource level.
Allow port 443 inbound — the response automatically comes back. No extra rule needed.

NACLs = stateless firewall at the subnet level.
Allow port 443 inbound — you must ALSO explicitly allow the response outbound.
Both inbound and outbound rules required. Always.

Most teams only configure Security Groups and forget NACLs exist.
At Citi Bank both were mandatory for every subnet. Defence in depth.

𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗿𝘂𝗹𝗲 𝗜 𝘂𝘀𝗲 𝗲𝘃𝗲𝗿𝘆𝘄𝗵𝗲𝗿𝗲:

Internet-facing resources — public subnet.
Everything else — private subnet.
Outbound internet from private — NAT Gateway.
Firewall at resource level — Security Groups.
Firewall at subnet level — NACLs.

Get these five right and your VPC will never be the reason for a 3am incident
